<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Guarino Guarini</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1624">1624</a>–<a href="/years/1683">1683</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Guarino Guarini was an Italian architect, mathematician and Theatine priest from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modena">Modena</a> who worked in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a> for the dukes of Savoy from 1666. His daring designs, based on complex geometry, include the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chapel-of-the-holy-shroud">Chapel of the Holy Shroud</a> with its cone of stacked arches, the church of San Lorenzo and the Palazzo Carignano. His treatise Architettura civile was published in 1737.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/guarino-guarini.jpg" alt="Guarino Guarini" width="320" height="533" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Guarino_Guarini2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Camillo Guarino Guarini was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modena">Modena</a> on 17 January 1624 into a deeply religious family; he and his four brothers all joined the Theatine Order. At fifteen he became a novice and was sent to the monastery of San Silvestro al Quirinale in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, where he stayed from 1639 to 1648 and studied the work of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francesco-borromini">Francesco Borromini</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gian-lorenzo-bernini">Gian Lorenzo Bernini</a> and Pietro da Cortona.</p><p>From Borromini he learned to use complex geometry as the basis of floor plans. He also trained in theology, philosophy and mathematics. Back in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modena">Modena</a> as an ordained priest in 1648, he became a lecturer in philosophy at the Theatine College two years later, and in 1649 he began his career as an architect for the Order with the rebuilding of San Vincenzo.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1655 Guarini was elected provost of the Theatine house in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modena">Modena</a> against the wishes of Alfonso IV d'Este, who banished him from the city. He reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/messina">Messina</a> in 1660, where he built the façade of Santa Maria Annunziata, and in 1665 he published in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> a large mathematical and philosophical treatise, the Placita Philosophica. When he arrived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a> towards the end of 1666 he submitted a new design for San Lorenzo, and work on it began in 1668. The church has a centralized nave and a dome of criss-crossing hyperbolic ribs, with windows of different shapes breaking up the surfaces between them.</p><p>In 1679 Guarini was commissioned by Prince Emmanuel Philibert of Carignano to build the Palazzo Carignano outside the medieval walls of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a>. It was completed in 1683 as a three-winged building, not the four wings first planned. Its brick façade is formed of two S-curves around a tall oval pavilion, an innovation in Italian Baroque palace design. He also built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chapel-of-the-holy-shroud">Chapel of the Holy Shroud</a> (1668-94), the Castle of Racconigi and the Colegio dei Nobili. He wrote on mathematics, astronomy, fortification and architecture, roughly a book every other year.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Guarini died on 6 March 1683. In 1686 the Theatines published 33 engraved plates of his designs, and in the 1730s, with the help of the architect Bernardo Antonio Vittone, his treatise Architettura civile appeared in 1737. In it he wrote that vaults are the main thing in architecture and that architecture aims at the gratification of the senses.</p><p>His diaphanous domes found no imitators among his contemporaries in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a>, and in the later 18th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neoclassicism">Neoclassicism</a> led to attacks such as Francesco Milizia's. His book was widely read in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and influenced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-bernhard-fischer-von-erlach">Johann Bernhard Fischer von Erlach</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-lukas-von-hildebrandt">Johann Lukas von Hildebrandt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balthasar-neumann">Balthasar Neumann</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Guarino Guarini” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The duchy acquired the Shroud of Turin in 1453, and the relic, moved from Chambéry to Turin in 1578, became a focus of dynastic devotion, housed from 1694 in <b>Guarino Guarini</b>’s Chapel of the Holy Shroud.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-savoy">Duchy of Savoy · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modena">Modena</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=44.65,10.93&amp;year=1662" aria-label="Modena on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4811 585 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Guarino Guarini"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5261 855h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5261 855h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=44.65,10.93&amp;year=1662">What was here in 1662? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
